    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Topography is the result of interaction between tectonic and geomorphic processes. Landform, especially offset stream channels, have been used to indicate slip along strike-slip faults. However,the geomorphic responses of the affected channels to strike-slip deformation have not received  much attention. In addition to direct tectonic offset, channels maybe deflected by shutter ridges blocking their courses, or, due to differential erosion, they may follow the main fault or secondary structures. All of these channel responses occur along the 50 km section of the San Andreas fault (SAF) in the Carrizo Plain, San Luis Obispo County, California (figure 1). Movement along secondary structures such as normal and reverse faults also affects the landforms. The quantitative investigation of channel and landform responses and the light it sheds on the tectonic process is the topic of this report.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
